0"} "Kaija Mustonen"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"] " / 10 February 1968 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/539"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Starter"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5004310"} "Saburo Takabayashi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/15004"} "Viktor Kapitonov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was the first women’s event in 1972, and although there was no clear favorite, several skaters were highly considered. This would include Soviet " [:a {:href "/athletes/82446"} "Nina Statkevich"] ", 1971 World Champion, who won the 1,500 at that event; Holland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/81478"} "Atje Keulen-Deelstra"] ", 1970 World Champion and recent winner of the 1972 European Championship, who had won the 1,500 at the Europeans; her teammate " [:a {:href "/athletes/80777"} "Stien Baas-Kaiser"] ", at her best in the distances, and the world record holder at 2:15.8, set in January 1971 in Davos; and American " [:a {:href "/athletes/81312"} "Dianne Holum"] ", who had won the 1,000 at the 1971 World Championships, and placed fourth overall at the Worlds in 1970 and 1971. Keulen-Deelstra was off in the first pair and set a mark to shoot at, 2:22.05. She waited for the fifth pair which featured Holum and Statkevich. They were together through 700 metres, and ahead of Keulen-Deelstra’s splits, but Holum then pulled away, finishing in 2:20.85, which would bring her the gold medal. Statkevich fell off, eventually finishing only sixth. Baas-Kaiser was in the 12th pair, but she started very slowly, and although she had the fastest final lap, she fell just short of catching Holum, finishing in 2:21.05 for a silver medal. Keulen-Deelstra’s time held up for the bronze. Holum would compete for only a few more weeks, at the 1972 World Sprints and World Championships, winning both 1,000s at the Sprints. She would then turn to coaching, later taking on a promising young Wisconsin skater named " [:a {:href "/athletes/86551"} "Eric Heiden"] ". Holum’s daughter, " [:a {:href "/athletes/99413"} "Kirstin"] ", would later skate for the US at the 1998 Nagano Olympics."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button864152", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button864152').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "300 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "700 m"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "1100 m"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "5O"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/81312"} "Dianne Holum"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "2:20.85"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "28.99"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "1:04.67"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "1:41.96"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "12O"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/80777"} "Stien Baas-Kaiser"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding